    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but it’s not just a \"moment\" though. it feels permanent. they sacrificed so much for a version of me that doesn't even exist anymore. i see how hard they work and then i look at myself and i just feel like a lie. like the only way to actually respect what they did is to just not be this person who failed them. it's like i'm just constant noise in an otherwise perfect story they tried to build.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The idea that you are a failure is a heavy narrative you've built around yourself, but it's not the reality of your life. Your parents' sacrifices were for *you*, the person you are right now, not for some idealized version. Instead of seeing yourself as noise, try to see yourself as someone who is still in the messy process of becoming. Even small steps forward, no matter how small, are a testament to the strength they invested in you.",
      "source": "target",
